AIE core clock, measured through the trace unit's timer -- Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G)
2026-09-07. env: mlir-aie ironenv (v1.4.2, llvm-aie 22.0.0.2026090201) + XRT SDK 2.21.75, native
Windows; driver 32.0.20101.3760; xrt-smi from C:\Windows\System32\AMD\xrt-smi.exe (XRT 2.21.0,
build 15e6319). One fresh process per power mode. Nothing on the device was changed except the
power mode, which was restored to `default` at the end (verified, last section).

Commands (from the ironenv; kernels/clock_probe/clock_probe.py --help has the setup):
  python3 kernels/clock_probe/clock_probe.py --label default --iters 10 --warmup 2 \
      --idle-sleep 5 --idle-repeats 3 --idle-burst 10
  for each of powersaver, balanced, performance, turbo:
    xrt-smi --batch configure --pmode <mode>
    python3 kernels/clock_probe/clock_probe.py --label <mode> --iters 5 --warmup 2 \
        --scalar-targets 1048576,2097152,4194304,8388608,16777216,33554432 \
        --vector-targets 1048576,4194304,16777216,67108864
  xrt-smi --batch configure --pmode default
  python3 kernels/clock_probe/clock_probe.py --label default-after-pmode-sweep (same points)
  python3 kernels/clock_probe/clock_probe.py --label default-no-trace --trace-size 0 --iters 5 \
      --warmup 2 --scalar-targets 1048576,4194304,16777216 --vector-targets 1048576,4194304,16777216

WHY: every per-second ceiling in docs/SILICON.md (TOPS per column, bytes per cycle on a
stream, MACs per cycle per core) multiplies a per-cycle figure by a clock nothing on this
machine had measured. RESEARCH.md cited 1.6 GHz from a web search; bottleneck_spatial_sweep
reasoned at 1 GHz and said so; xrt-smi prints no clock. SILICON.md 1.7 carried the clock as
unmeasured and objective S0 was this measurement.

METHOD: one Worker on one core tile (logical Tile(0,2)) runs event0(); a DMA-free loop of
`iters` iterations; event1(). The tile's trace unit stamps both instruction events with its
timer and streams the packets to a host buffer through IRON's Program.enable_trace. The host
times the same call with the runtime's own submit+wait bracket (KernelResult.npu_time, the
'hw' column, the same bracket dispatch_floor_npu.log used) and fits
    hw_ms = intercept + slope * (stamp1 - stamp0)     across 2^18..2^25 iterations,
so the fixed per-dispatch cost lands in the intercept and the clock is 1/slope. Two loops:
'scalar' (a dependent add on a volatile local) and 'vector' (a dependent 16-lane int32
aie::add chain); they must fit to the same clock. Every call is verified before its timing
is kept (mode/target echo, checksum of the loop's arithmetic, core-tile row, the real event
pair present ahead of the filler pairs). Compile is excluded (warmup + @iron.jit cache).
The 'wall' column is
meaningless with trace on -- IRON allocates a 64 KB trace buffer and dumps it to disk inside
that bracket -- and is not fitted.

RESULT (core clock = 1/slope of the hardware-bracket fit; scalar loop / vector loop):
  power mode      clock (GHz)        R^2                   fit intercept (us, hw, trace on)
  default         1.7983 / 1.7924    1.000000 / 0.999995   347 / 321
  powersaver      0.7985 / 0.7984    1.000000 / 1.000000   524 / 462
  balanced        1.0274 / 1.0278    1.000000 / 1.000000   395 / 384
  performance     1.8002 / 1.7986    0.999999 / 1.000000   397 / 323
  turbo           1.7998 / 1.7989    1.000000 / 1.000000   304 / 282
  default, after  1.7990 / 1.7993    0.999999 / 1.000000   (re-measured after the sweep)
The two loops agree within 0.33% (default) and 0.01-0.09% (every other mode). The raw
ratio cycles/hw at the longest point (168 ms) reads 1.7947 GHz in default, converging on
the fit from below as the intercept amortizes.

WHAT ELSE THE SAME RUNS SHOW:
- Cycles per iteration are exactly constant at every length: scalar 9.000, vector 2.000,
  from 2^18 to 2^25 (2^26 for vector) iterations. That is what makes the stamps trustworthy
  as core cycles: a timer at a different rate would not give integer cycle counts for both
  loops, and it pins the sync-frame quantum below at exactly 2^18.
- Idle probe (default): three calls each after 5 s of idle read 1.7851 / 1.7716 / 1.7683 GHz,
  the ten back-to-back calls after them 1.759-1.787. No idle penalty at the 5 s scale, so
  the session-to-session latency drift docs/DECISIONS.md records is not an idle clock
  state at this timescale. Power mode, however, is a 2.25x lever (0.80 -> 1.80 GHz).
- pyxrt device.get_info(max_clock_frequency_mhz) reads 800 in EVERY mode, including the
  ones measured at 1.80 GHz. It equals the powersaver clock, not the live one.
- The trace packet header says the traced core is at row 2, column 1, and get_coreid()
  inside the kernel says the same: IRON's logical column 0 is physical column 1 on this
  xclbin, consistent with the 4x4 overlay occupying columns 1-4 (docs/SILICON.md 1.1).
- `xrt-smi configure --pmode turbo` printed '[xrt-smi] ERROR: Failed to escape
  (0xc0000001): A device attached to the system is not functioning.', yet the platform
  report then read 'Turbo' and the clock measured the same 1.80 GHz as performance and
  default. Restoring default from turbo printed the same error and the report then read
  'Default'; the device kept working (last section). powersaver, balanced and performance
  switched without error. Whether turbo is a real fourth state on this part is unresolved.
- Trace overhead, measured (last section, --trace-size 0 at three matching points): the
  same design's untraced hardware intercept is 281.9 us (scalar) / 210.6 us (vector),
  40-110 us above the no-compute passthrough's 169.8 us (dispatch_floor_npu.log) -- a
  core to load and start -- and trace adds 65-110 us on top (346.9 / 321.4 us traced).
  Both cancel in the fit. The untraced slopes, 5.0006 and 1.1156 ns per iteration, with
  the traced 9 and 2 cycles per iteration, give 1.7998 and 1.7928 GHz: the clock a third
  way, from timing alone.

TOOLING, found on the way (all on this machine, this toolchain):
- Peano (llvm-aie 22) declares get_cycles() in its aie_api compat header but never
  defines it: aie::tile::current().cycles() links with 'ld.lld: error: undefined symbol:
  get_cycles()'. __builtin_readcyclecounter() dies in the legalizer ('unable to legalize
  instruction: G_READCYCLECOUNTER'), and inline asm dies in IRTranslator ('unable to
  translate instruction: call'). The trace unit is the one path to the tile timer the open
  toolchain exposes -- so this is also the first end-to-end hardware trace on npu1 on this
  machine (docs/SILICON.md objective S2's first step).
- One event0/event1 pair alone never reaches host memory: the trace unit packs frames into
  32-byte packets and the shim S2MM DMA writes 64-byte bursts (mlir-aie programming_guide
  section-4b: 'a simple core may have too few events to create a valid trace packet'). The
  kernel emits 256 filler pairs after the real pair; the host reads the first pair only.
- mlir-aie v1.4.2's aie.utils.trace.parse mis-times any gap longer than 2^18 cycles. The
  hardware encodes such a gap as an Event_Sync frame (0xff, one wrap of the 18-bit delta
  counter) followed by a Repeat frame counting further wraps; parse.py treats 0xff as a
  timer no-op and the Repeat as re-issuing the last *event*, so a 2,097,172-cycle gap
  came back as 45,017 with eight spurious INSTR_EVENT_0 entries. clock_probe.py decodes
  the frames itself (Sync = +2^18, Repeat repeats whatever preceded it) and cross-checks
  against parse.py on a run short enough to contain no sync frame: both read 73,732
  cycles. At 1.8 GHz the upstream limit is 146 us between consecutive events -- any real
  kernel trace on this machine will hit it.
- `xrt-smi configure --pmode` works from an unelevated shell on this driver.

CAVEATS:
- The measurement assumes the trace timer ticks at the core clock. The integer cycles per
  iteration of two different loops support it; it is still an assumption.
- One core tile only (logical column 0, row 2). Other tiles and columns were not measured
  and are assumed to share the clock domain. The 'run under a concurrent VitisAI EP
  session' leg of objective S0 was NOT run: this worktree has no models/ directory.
- The clock while a *design* is running is what was measured; between dispatches the
  hardware may sit lower (the 800 MHz XRT reports could be that state) -- the 5 s idle
  probe says any such transition is over before a 42 ms call has finished.
- Nothing here changes any latency number in the repo. It changes their denominators:
  every '% of peak at 1.6 GHz' in docs/SILICON.md is now '% of peak at 1.8 GHz' by the
  ratio 1.6/1.8 = 0.889.

